<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/expert/ethics-of-technology-and-ai">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Ethics of technology and AI is philosophy's newest applied branch — and its most urgent:
    <strong>systems that make decisions about people demand answers to questions philosophers
    have worked on for centuries</strong> (responsibility, fairness, moral status, autonomy) at a
    pace the tradition never anticipated. The unit's applied-ethics topics — stealing, simulated
    killing, eating animals, telling lies — turn out to map onto the new technologies almost
    one-to-one: data theft and training-data scraping, violence in games and simulations,
    cultivated meat and algorithmic farming, AI deception and deepfakes. Each section below runs
    the classical machinery — utilitarianism (consequences, impartially aggregated), Kantian
    deontology (universalisable duties, persons as ends), virtue ethics (character) and the
    meta-ethical question (are moral claims facts at all?) — against its technological heir.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="alignment as ethics, machine moral status, responsibility gaps, and power" widget="equationStep" config={techCfg} />

  <h2>Stealing: property, data and the AI training problem</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The classical analysis.</strong> Kant's test: can "take others' property when it
    suits me" be universalised? No — universalised theft abolishes the property institution the
    act relies on, so theft fails the categorical imperative outright. Utilitarianism is more
    conditional: theft is wrong because property rules create the incentives (investment,
    creation, exchange) whose collapse harms everyone — but a theft that truly helps the
    needy at trivial cost to the owner is exactly the case the calculus makes difficult (the
    shop-with-unlocked-door hypothetical is the A-level classic). Virtue ethics asks what the
    act makes of the agent: a thief is practising dishonesty as a trait.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where property itself comes from.</strong> The applied debate rests on a theory of
    ownership, and the tradition supplies one: Locke's labour theory — mixing work with
    unowned things creates title, provided "enough and as good" is left for others (the proviso
    that every modern appropriation argument quietly reopens). Copyright is younger than most
    assume: the Statute of Anne (1710) created authors' rights as an explicit bargain for
    learning — incentive, not natural right — which is why each new copying technology
    (printing, radio, video, sampling, model training) relitigates the same utilitarian trade.
    The scrapers' defence ("learning like a student") and the publishers' reply ("market
    substitution without payment") are the 1710 bargain's terms restated at industrial scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The technological heir.</strong> Copyrighted works scraped to train large models
    (the New York Times sued OpenAI and Microsoft over exactly this, December 2023) force the
    classical debate into a new shape: is copying training data "stealing" when the copy is
    statistical rather than substitutive? Kantians argue the creator's consent is bypassed
    (treated as a means); utilitarians must weigh a genuine innovation externality against
    creators' incentive collapse; and everyone confronts the meta-ethical oddity that "property
    in data" is a category law is inventing in real time. The disagreement is not about facts —
    it is about which analogy (theft, learning, library) is morally load-bearing.
  </p>

  <h2>Simulated killing: games, films and the corruption question</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The classical analysis.</strong> Plato (in the Republic) wanted poets censored:
    representation trains the soul. Aristotle replied with catharsis: drama purges emotion
    rather than exciting it. Utilitarianism makes it empirical: does simulated killing cause
    real harm? The research verdict is genuinely unsettled — early reviews (including the APA's
    2015 statement) found small links to aggression measures, while better-designed later work
    (Przybylski and Vuorre, 2020, using adolescent data across many countries) found no
    relationship between game time and aggressive behaviour. Kant's test is brisk: "pretend to
    kill in a game" universalises harmlessly — no one is deprived of anything. Virtue ethics
    keeps the hardest question: does rehearsing cruelty habituate the character, even if no
    victim exists? Moral psychology's partial answers — desensitisation effects are measurable
    and modest; moral disengagement (Bandura) explains how players compartmentalise.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The technological heir.</strong> VR raises the stakes of the same question
    (immersion without a victim), and the simulation frame cuts the other way too: if
    simulated worlds ever hosted real experiencers, the ethics of "just a game" would invert —
    a thought experiment that previews the machine moral-status debate below.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the research settled long ago that violent games cause real
    violence." It did not: the literature is contested end to end — meta-analyses disagree
    about effect sizes, critics document publication bias and weak aggression measures, and the
    best-controlled large studies find no meaningful behavioural relationship. The
    philosophically interesting residue is precisely the unsettled part: whether moral risk
    requires demonstrated harm or only plausible habituation — which is the same dispute
    utilitarianism and virtue ethics have always had, now wearing a controller.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The power dimension: bias at scale.</strong> The widget's fourth theme is where
    the classical theories meet structural critique. ProPublica's 2016 investigation of the
    COMPAS recidivism score found Black defendants flagged high-risk at roughly twice the
    false-positive rate of white defendants (about 45% versus 23%) — while the vendor's own
    metric (error balance across groups) was satisfied: the mathematics cannot satisfy every
    fairness definition at once (Kleinberg and colleagues proved the impossibility), so
    "fair algorithm" is a policy choice wearing a formula. Cambridge Analytica's harvesting
    of up to 87 million Facebook profiles for political targeting (2016–18) showed data
    expropriation functioning as electoral infrastructure. And the Moral Machine experiment
    (Awad and colleagues, 2018: 40 million moral decisions from millions of people in 233
    countries and territories) mapped how differently cultures rank lives in unavoidable
    crash scenarios — empirical evidence that value loading inherits real, measurable moral
    disagreement.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "AI ethics is about a future superintelligence, so it can
    wait." The near-term cases are present-tense: recidivism scores, hiring filters, credit
    models, content moderation, autonomous-vehicle crash logic. The long-run alignment
    problem and today's discriminatory classifier are the same discipline at different
    horizons — both are questions about encoding values in optimisers, and the short-horizon
    versions are where the affected people are living.
  </p>

  <h2>Eating animals: suffering, status and cultivated meat</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The classical analysis.</strong> Utilitarianism (Singer's <em>Animal
    Liberation</em>, 1975) makes species membership morally irrelevant: suffering counts
    whoever suffers, and the scale is industrial — more than 70 billion land animals slaughtered
    annually, most in confinement systems. Kant denies animals direct moral standing (they are
    not rational legislators), but his indirect-duties route — cruelty to animals corrodes
    humanity in us — still condemns factory farming by consequence. Virtue ethics asks what
    factory-farm complicity makes of the eater (callousness), and contractarian denials
    (animals cannot reciprocate) run into the "marginal cases" argument: infants and the
    severely cognitively impaired also cannot contract, yet retain standing — so the
    rationality criterion cannot be doing the work its defenders want.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The technological heir.</strong> Cultivated meat (approved first in Singapore,
    2020; then the US, 2023) promises the utilitarian dream — real meat without the animal —
    and re-prices the debate from principles to economics. Meanwhile AI-optimised farming
    sharpens the efficiency side, and the same suffering-criterion logic points forward:
    if moral status tracks the capacity to suffer rather than species, the question of machine
    suffering is not a joke but the next case on the docket.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Law is catching up to the argument.</strong> The suffering criterion is entering
    statute: the UK's Animal Welfare (Sentience) Act 2022 legally recognised vertebrates —
    and, unusually, decapod crustaceans and cephalopod molluscs — as sentient, a direct
    response to the crustacean-welfare literature (documented pain responses in crabs and
    octopuses). The practical implication is immediate: boiling lobsters alive, unremarkable
    for centuries, became reviewable practice because a philosophical criterion — capacity to
    suffer, not species membership — became law. The Singer position winning by instalments,
    while the underlying argument remains officially unwon, is exactly how applied ethics
    usually advances.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "utilitarianism is the selfish 'ends justify means'
    doctrine." Classical utilitarianism is radically <em>impartial</em>: everyone's welfare
    counts equally, including strangers and (in Singer's version) animals. The doctrine's
    disturbing cases (organ harvesting, the trolley problem) come from its impartiality, not
    from selfishness.
  </p>

  <h2>Telling lies: from Kant's murderer to the deepfake</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The classical analysis.</strong> Kant is absolutist: lying cannot be universalised
    (a world where lying is permissible destroys the trust lying exploits), and even the
    famous case — lying to the murderer at the door (Benjamin Constant's 1797 challenge) —
    gets Kant's refusal, with a chilling corollary: if the lie misdirects the murderer and the
    victim is found, the liar shares responsibility. Utilitarians bite that bullet gladly:
    the lie to the murderer is obviously right, and deception is wrong only on net —
    which makes the theory flexible and, critics say, unguidable in advance. Virtue ethics
    locates the cost in character and trust: the honest agent is one whose word carries weight.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The technological heir.</strong> AI scales every form of deception: generative
    models "hallucinate" confidently; deepfake audio meddled in Slovakia's 2023 election;
    Microsoft's 2016 chatbot Tay turned racist within roughly 16 hours of deployment, showing
    that systems mirror their inputs rather than moralising. The philosophical shift: deception
    no longer needs an intender — a model can mislead without meaning anything, which strains
    both Kant's will-based analysis and the law's fraud categories. The meta-ethical point
    underneath: if moral claims are not facts (see below), there is no fact of the matter for a
    machine to load — value alignment inherits whatever meta-ethics turns out to be true.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "Kant's ban on lying is a strawman nobody defends." The
    murderer-at-the-door exchange (Constant 1797; Kant's reply "On a Supposed Right to Lie",
    1797) is a live debate in the journals, defended on the grounds that a rule permitting
    convenient lying gives every agent a permanent escape hatch. Whatever one concludes, the
    position is argued, not a caricature.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Meta-ethics decides the engineering.</strong> Is "this action is wrong" a truth-
    apt claim (cognitivism: moral realism, or Mackie's error theory — claims aim at facts that
    do not exist), or an expression of attitude (Ayer's emotivism, non-cognitivism)? This is
    not classroom decoration: value alignment is literally the project of loading values into
    optimisers, and its difficulty changes completely on each meta-ethical picture. Realism
    suggests values are there to be discovered; error theory suggests they must be invented and
    imposed (a political act); emotivism suggests the whole framing is confused. Bostrom's
    orthogonality thesis (intelligence and final goals are independent) and the instrumental
    convergence arguments (most goals drive agents to seek resources and self-preservation)
    make the loading problem hard on any picture — the meta-ethics decides what "hard" means.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Moral status and responsibility gaps.</strong> If suffering or experience is the
    criterion of moral status, current models almost certainly lack it — but the boundary is
    unknown and "we will know it when we see it" is not a policy; the uncertainty itself is
    now discussed seriously (graduated status, precautionary duties). Responsibility gaps
    (Matthias, 2004): when learning systems act in ways no designer foresaw, neither coder,
    owner nor user seems fully responsible — the EU AI Act (2024) responds with risk-tiered
    obligations, and the legal doctrine is being invented in real time. Meaningful human
    control (the Dutch drones debate's standard: information, time and authority to
    intervene) is the most operational answer so far.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can moral disagreement across cultures be reconciled by
    any single loaded value set (moral pluralism versus lowest-common-denominator principles)?
    Does scale itself create new duties (systemic risk, epistemic pollution at population
    level)? And who deliberates — alignment as a political problem of who decides, not just a
    technical problem of what to encode? The tradition's verdicts differ; that is the point of
    running them against the new cases.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI (2021); EU AI Act (2024); Friedman & Hendry, Value Sensitive Design (2019); Vallor, Technology and the Virtues (2016).</p>
</TopicPage>
